B)\">18.06</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\">8</td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">9.03</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td data-h=\"20 log (dB)\">20.00</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\">10</td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">10.00</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td data-h=\"20 log (dB)\">26.02</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\">20</td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">13.01</td></tr><tr><td>31.62</td><td data-h=\"20 log (dB)\">30.00</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\">31.62</td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">15.00</td></tr><tr><td>50</td><td data-h=\"20 log (dB)\">33.98</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\">50</td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">16.99</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td data-h=\"20 log (dB)\">40.00</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\">100</td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">20.00</td></tr><tr><td></td><td data-h=\"20 log (dB)\">60.00</td><td data-h=\"Power ratio\" class=\"e\"></td><td data-h=\"10 log (dB)\">30.00</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style=\"color:#14532d\">★ ×2 voltage = +6 dB, ×10 = +20 dB, ×0.707 = −3 dB. −20 dB/decade = −6 dB/octave per pole.</p>",[]],"1684":["<div class=\"rb\" style=\"background:#ebe3fb;border-color:#c4b2ee\"><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\" style=\"--hc:#7a6e94\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"color:#fff;background:#7a4fd0;padding:0 .3em;border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\">9</td><td style=\"font-weight:700\">Powers of 2 Bits, counters, ADC</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"rb nb\" style=\"background:#fff6d6\"><p style=\"color:#5a3d00\"><b>Used for:</b> ADC/DAC resolution V/(2ⁿ − 1), number of bits for N levels, counter modulus, memory size, Gray/Johnson counters.</p></div><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\" style=\"--hc:#7a4fd0\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background:#7a4fd0\">n</th><th style=\"background:#7a4fd0\">2ⁿ</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>4</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>8</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>16</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>64</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>128</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>256</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>512</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>1,024</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>2,048</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>4,096</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>8,192</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>16,384</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>32,768</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>65,536</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>131,072</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>262,144</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>524,288</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>1,048,576</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>2,097,152</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>4,194,304</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>8,388,608</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>16,777,216</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style=\"color:#14532d\">★ 2¹⁰ = 1024 ≈ 10³; 8-bit = 256 levels (255 steps); 12-bit = 4096; 1 K memory = 1024 bytes.</p><div class=\"rb\" style=\"background:#f1e4da;border-color:#d7b9a3\"><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"color:#fff;background:#8d5a3b;padding:0 .3em;border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\">10</td><td style=\"font-weight:700\">Squares and cubes Quick arithmetic</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"rb nb\" style=\"background:#fff6d6\"><p style=\"color:#5a3d00\"><b>Used for:</b> I²R losses, V²/R power, a² impedance referral, (kV)² in per-unit base, transformer loss scaling x².</p></div><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt rc\" style=\"--hc:#8d5a3b\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background:#8d5a3b\">n</th><th style=\"background:#8d5a3b\">n²</th><th style=\"background:#8d5a3b\">n³</th><th style=\"background:#8d5a3b\">n</th><th style=\"background:#8d5a3b\">n²</th><th style=\"background:#8d5a3b\">n³</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>11</td><td data-h=\"n²\">121</td><td data-h=\"n³\">1331</td><td data-h=\"n\">21</td><td data-h=\"n²\">441</td><td data-h=\"n³\">9261</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td data-h=\"n²\">144</td><td data-h=\"n³\">1728</td><td data-h=\"n\">22</td><td data-h=\"n²\">484</td><td data-h=\"n³\">10648</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td data-h=\"n²\">169</td><td data-h=\"n³\">2197</td><td data-h=\"n\">23</td><td data-h=\"n²\">529</td><td data-h=\"n³\">12167</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td data-h=\"n²\">196</td><td data-h=\"n³\">2744</td><td data-h=\"n\">24</td><td data-h=\"n²\">576</td><td data-h=\"n³\">13824</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td data-h=\"n²\">225</td><td data-h=\"n³\">3375</td><td data-h=\"n\">25</td><td data-h=\"n²\">625</td><td data-h=\"n³\">15625</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td data-h=\"n²\">256</td><td data-h=\"n³\">4096</td><td data-h=\"n\">26</td><td data-h=\"n²\">676</td><td data-h=\"n³\">17576</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td data-h=\"n²\">289</td><td data-h=\"n³\">4913</td><td data-h=\"n\">27</td><td data-h=\"n²\">729</td><td data-h=\"n³\">19683</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td data-h=\"n²\">324</td><td data-h=\"n³\">5832</td><td data-h=\"n\">28</td><td data-h=\"n²\">784</td><td data-h=\"n³\">21952</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td data-h=\"n²\">361</td><td data-h=\"n³\">6859</td><td data-h=\"n\">29</td><td data-h=\"n²\">841</td><td data-h=\"n³\">24389</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td data-h=\"n²\">400</td><td data-h=\"n³\">8000</td><td data-h=\"n\">30</td><td data-h=\"n²\">900</td><td data-h=\"n³\">27000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",[]],"1685":["<div class=\"rb\" style=\"background:#fde1e5;border-color:#f0b0b7\"><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"color:#fff;background:#d63a4a;padding:0 .3em;border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\">11</td><td style=\"font-weight:700\">Numbers with π, √2, √3 Where 4.44, 1.11, 0.9, 1.35, 9.55 come from</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"rb nb\" style=\"background:#fff6d6\"><p style=\"color:#5a3d00\"><b>Used for:</b> Rectifier averages, form factor, EMF equation, torque from rpm, angular frequency at 50 Hz, field constants.</p></div><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\" style=\"--hc:#d63a4a\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background:#d63a4a\">Expression</th><th style=\"background:#d63a4a\">Value</th><th style=\"background:#d63a4a\">Used in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>π</td><td>3.14159</td><td>circles, ω = 2πf</td></tr><tr><td>2π</td><td>6.28319</td><td>ω = 2πf</td></tr><tr><td>π/2</td><td>1.5708</td><td>90° in rad</td></tr><tr><td>1/π</td><td>0.31831</td><td>half-wave average V_m/π</td></tr><tr><td>2/π</td><td>0.63662</td><td>full-wave average 2V_m/π</td></tr><tr><td>1/2π</td><td>0.159155</td><td>f = 1/(2π√LC)</td></tr><tr><td>100π</td><td>314.159</td><td>ω at 50 Hz</td></tr><tr><td>4/π</td><td>1.27324</td><td>square-wave fundamental</td></tr><tr><td>2√2/π</td><td>0.900316</td><td>rectifier V_dc = 0.9 V_rms</td></tr><tr><td>3√2/π</td><td>1.35047</td><td>3-φ bridge 1.35 V_L</td></tr><tr><td>π/(2√2)</td><td>1.11072</td><td>form factor 1.11</td></tr><tr><td>2π/√2</td><td>4.44288</td><td>EMF constant 4.44</td></tr><tr><td>60/2π</td><td>9.5493</td><td>T = 9.55 P/N</td></tr><tr><td>μ₀ = 4π×10⁻⁷</td><td>1.25664e-06</td><td>H/m</td></tr><tr><td>1/(4πε₀)</td><td>8.988e+09</td><td>Coulomb constant ≈ 9 × 10⁹</td></tr><tr><td>e</td><td>2.71828</td><td>natural base</td></tr><tr><td>1 rad</td><td>57.2958</td><td>degrees</td></tr><tr><td>1°</td><td>0.0174533</td><td>radians</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",[]],"1686":["<div class=\"rb\" style=\"background:#dcf1df;border-color:#a9dbb0\"><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"color:#fff;background:#2e8b57;padding:0 .3em;border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\">12</td><td style=\"font-weight:700\">Shortcut rules to build any value Make missing values from the anchors</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"rb nb\" style=\"background:#fff6d6\"><p style=\"color:#5a3d00\"><b>Used for:</b> When a value is not in the tables: build it from anchors using these rules.</p></div><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\" style=\"--hc:#2e8b57\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background:#2e8b57\">Rule</th><th style=\"background:#2e8b57\">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ln(ab) = ln a + ln b</td><td>ln 50 = ln 5 + ln 10 = 3.912</td></tr><tr><td>ln(a/b) = ln a − ln b</td><td>ln 1.5 = 1.099 − 0.693 = 0.405</td></tr><tr><td>ln aⁿ = n ln a</td><td>ln 1000 = 3 × 2.303 = 6.908</td></tr><tr><td>e^(a+b) = eᵃ·eᵇ</td><td>e^1.5 = 2.718 × 1.649 = 4.482</td></tr><tr><td>eˣ ≈ 1 + x (small x)</td><td>e^0.05 ≈ 1.05 (exact 1.0513)</td></tr><tr><td>√(1 + x) ≈ 1 + x/2</td><td>√1.1 ≈ 1.05 (exact 1.0488)</td></tr><tr><td>(1 + x)ⁿ ≈ 1 + nx</td><td>1.02¹⁰ ≈ 1.2 (exact 1.219)</td></tr><tr><td>sin x ≈ tan x ≈ x (rad, small)</td><td>sin 5° ≈ 0.0873 (exact 0.0872)</td></tr><tr><td>cos x ≈ 1 − x²/2</td><td>cos 10° ≈ 0.9848</td></tr><tr><td>log₁₀ x = ln x / 2.303</td><td>log 50 = 3.912/2.303 = 1.699</td></tr><tr><td>Half-life t½ = 0.693τ</td><td>τ = 10 ms → 6.93 ms</td></tr><tr><td>n time constants → e⁻ⁿ left</td><td>4τ → 1.8 % left</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",[]],"1687":["<div class=\"rb\" style=\"background:#fdeec4;border-color:#efcf7c\"><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"color:#fff;background:#e39b17;padding:0 .3em;border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\">13</td><td style=\"font-weight:700\">Formula in doubt? Values where it doesn&#x27;t matter If every value is 2 (or 1, 0, 45°), plus and multiply give the same answer</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"rb nb\" style=\"background:#fff6d6\"><p style=\"color:#5a3d00\"><b>Used for:</b> the exam moment when you cannot recall whether a formula adds or multiplies, squares or doubles, or which way up a ratio goes.</p></div><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\" style=\"--hc:#e39b17\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background:#e39b17;color:#1b2a4a\">Identity</th><th style=\"background:#e39b17;color:#1b2a4a\">What it means for you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2 + 2 = 2 × 2 = 2² = 4</td><td>With 2, add / multiply / square all give 4 — if every number in the sum is 2, either form gives the same answer</td></tr><tr><td>x² = 2x only at x = 2</td><td>Confused between x² and 2x? At x = 2 both are 4 — answer is safe; for any other value you must know which</td></tr><tr><td>x + x = 2x ; x · x = x²</td><td>Same value added → ×2; same value multiplied → square</td></tr><tr><td>1ⁿ = 1 ; 1 × a = a ; √1 = 1</td><td>With 1, powers and roots vanish — a formula error in the exponent cannot change the answer</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>0 × anything = 0 ; e⁰ = 1 ; ln 1 =</p><p>Zero / one values kill terms — check what remains</p><p>0</p><p>Ratio upside-down? If the two values are equal the answer is</p><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td>a/b = b/a only when a = b</td><td>1 either way</td></tr><tr><td>R ∥ R = R/2 ; R + R = 2R</td><td>Equal resistors: series doubles, parallel halves</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>cos 45° = sin 45° = 0.707 ; tan</p><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td>45° = 1</td><td>At 45° sin and cos are interchangeable; R = X gives 45°</td></tr><tr><td>√2 × √2 = 2 ; √3 × √3 = 3</td><td>RMS × √2 and line ÷ √3 cancel neatly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style=\"color:#14532d\">★ If the given numbers make both forms equal (all values 2, equal R&#x27;s, 45°, 1 or 0), choose either — the answer is the same. But never use 2 to TEST a formula you are unsure of: it hides the mistake. Test with 3 instead (3 + 3 = 6 but 3 × 3 = 9).</p>",[]],"1688":["<div class=\"rb\" style=\"background:#fde1e5;border-color:#f0b0b7\"><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt\"><tbody><tr><td style=\"color:#fff;background:#d63a4a;padding:0 .3em;border-radius:4px;font-weight:700\">14</td><td style=\"font-weight:700\">Formula in doubt? 9 quick tests that settle it Units, extremes, equal values, limits, symmetry</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div><div class=\"rb nb\" style=\"background:#fff6d6\"><p style=\"color:#5a3d00\"><b>Used for:</b> deciding between two half-remembered formulas in under 30 seconds, and catching an upside-down ratio before you mark the option.</p></div><div class=\"rtw\"><table class=\"rt rc\" style=\"--hc:#d63a4a\"><thead><tr><th style=\"background:#d63a4a\">Test</th><th style=\"background:#d63a4a\">How to apply</th><th style=\"background:#d63a4a\">Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Units / dimensions</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">Both sides must have the same unit. P = V²/R gives W ✓; P = V/R² gives A/Ω ✗</td><td data-h=\"Example\">√(L/C) is Ω ✓ ; √(LC) is s ✓ (time) — so 1/√(LC) is rad/s</td></tr><tr><td>Extreme value: put 0</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">R = 0 or X = 0 — does the answer make physical sense?</td><td data-h=\"Example\">Series R‖C at R = 0 must give pure capacitor</td></tr><tr><td>Extreme value: put ∞</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">Open circuit / very large value</td><td data-h=\"Example\">R<sub>L</sub> → ∞ in a divider → output = full source voltage</td></tr><tr><td>Equal values</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">Two equal items must share equally</td><td data-h=\"Example\">Parallel generators of equal X share load 50 : 50</td></tr><tr><td>Known special case</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">Formula must reduce to the textbook case</td><td data-h=\"Example\">Three-phase P = √3 V<sub>L</sub>I<sub>L</sub> cos φ must equal 3 V<sub>ph</sub>I<sub>ph</sub> cos φ (star: V<sub>L</sub> = √3 V<sub>ph</sub>)</td></tr><tr><td>Bigger / smaller check</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">Does increasing an input push the answer the right way?</td><td data-h=\"Example\">More load → more Cu loss; higher X → lower P<sub>max</sub></td></tr><tr><td>Limits 0 ≤ answer ≤ 1</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">pf, efficiency, slip (motor), probability, ζ for underdamped</td><td data-h=\"Example\">η = 1.2 or pf = 1.5 means the formula is upside-down</td></tr><tr><td>Symmetry</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">Swapping two identical items must not change the result</td><td data-h=\"Example\">Z₁ ∥ Z₂ formula is symmetric in Z₁, Z₂ ✓</td></tr><tr><td>Check with 3, not 2</td><td data-h=\"How to apply\">3 separates the forms: 3 + 3 = 6, but 3 × 3 = 3² = 9 (with 2 all give 4)</td><td data-h=\"Example\">Pick 3 (or 10) when testing which form is right</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style=\"color:#14532d\">★ Fastest order: units → put 0 or ∞ → equal values. One of the three usually kills the wrong option.</p>",[]]}